    "question": "Melony makes $21 of profit every time she sells 3 shirts and four times as much profit when she sells two pairs of sandals. How much profit will she make if she sells 7 shirts and 3 pairs of sandals?",
    "gold": "175",
    "answer": "Melony makes $21 * 4 = $<<21*4=84>>84 of profit for two pairs of sandals.\nMelony makes $84 / 2 pairs = $<<84/2=42>>42 of profit per pair of shoes.\nMelony makes $21 / 3 shirts = $<<21/3=7>>7 of profit per shirt.\nFor seven shirts, Melony will make 7 shirts * $7 = $<<7*7=49>>49 of profit.\nFor three pairs of sandals, Melony will make 3 pairs * $42/pair = $<<3*42=126>>126 of profit.\nMelony will make a total of $49 + $126 = $<<49+126=175>>175 of profit.\n#### 175"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine the profit Melony makes from selling 7 shirts and 3 pairs of sandals, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the profit from selling 3 shirts.\n2. Determine the profit from selling 2 pairs of sandals.\n3. Calculate the total profit from selling 7 shirts.\n4. Calculate the total profit from selling 3 pairs of sandals.\n5. Sum the profits from steps 3 and 4 to get the final answer.\n\nLet's start with the first step:\n\n1. The profit from selling 3 shirts is $21. Therefore, the profit from selling 1 shirt is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{21}{3} = 7 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n   So, the profit from selling 7 shirts is:\n   \\[\n   7 \\times 7 = 49 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\n2. The profit from selling 2 pairs of sandals is four times the profit from selling 3 shirts, which is:\n   \\[\n   4 \\times 21 = 84 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n   So, the profit from selling 3 pairs of sandals is:\n   \\[\n   84 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\n3. The total profit from selling 7 shirts is $49.\n\n4. The total profit from selling 3 pairs of sandals is $84.\n\n5. The final profit is the sum of the profits from selling the shirts and the sandals:\n   \\[\n   49 + 84 = 133 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{133}\n\\]",
